Material from other sources

The products are derived from data of other providers, which are not redistributed here and whose
terms continue to apply to them. The products are an independent work by the author; they are not
products of these providers and have not been reviewed or endorsed by them.

- Hypocentres: unified hypocenter catalogue of the Japan Meteorological Agency, compiled from data
  of JMA, the National Research Institute for Earth Science and Disaster Resilience, universities
  and other institutions. Source: Japan Meteorological Agency website
  (https://www.data.jma.go.jp/eqev/data/bulletin/hypo.html). Created by F. Ikegami based on this
  catalogue (events selected, declustered and model-fitted by the author).
- Slab-top depths used for the slab mask and the band slab_top_km: Slab2 (Hayes et al. 2018, U.S.
  Geological Survey) and the plate-configuration models distributed by F. Hirose (Meteorological
  Research Institute).
- Coastlines in the figures: GSHHG (Wessel & Smith 1996), GNU Lesser General Public License.
- Files under thermal/: calibrated against onshore heat-flow observations of the Global Heat Flow
  Database, Release 2021 (Fuchs, Norden & International Heat Flow Commission 2021, GFZ Data
  Services, https://doi.org/10.5880/fidgeo.2021.014, CC BY 4.0); node medians of these observations
  are plotted in the figures. The strain-rate term and the band strain_rate_nstrain_per_yr are
  derived from the gridded strain-rate field of Ohtate et al. (2026, Earth Planets Space 78:210,
  https://doi.org/10.1186/s40623-026-02475-y, CC BY 4.0); the author computed the second invariant,
  regridded it to 0.1 degree and smoothed it. The Curie-point depth model of Li et al. (2017,
  https://doi.org/10.1038/srep45129) is used for a comparison of regional medians only.
